1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,539
Book Three, Proposition Eighteen.

2
00:00:02,539 --> 00:00:13,517
If a straight line touch a circle, and a straight line be joined from the center to the point of contact, the straight line so joined will be perpendicular to the tangent.

3
00:00:13,517 --> 00:00:22,634
Let the straight line D E touch the circle A B C at the point C, let the center F of circle A B C be taken, and let F C be joined from F to C.

4
00:00:22,634 --> 00:00:25,330
We claim that F C is perpendicular to D E.

5
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:34,200
For suppose it is not, and if possible, let F G be drawn from F perpendicular to D E, meeting the circle at B.

6
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:41,677
Then in triangle F C G, since angle F G C is right, by One Seventeen angle F C G is acute.

7
00:00:41,677 --> 00:00:49,519
Because the greater angle is subtended by the greater side, by One Nineteen F C is greater than F G.

8
00:00:49,519 --> 00:01:00,262
But F C equals F B as radii of circle A B C; therefore F B is also greater than F G—the part greater than the whole, which is impossible.

9
00:01:00,262 --> 00:01:08,339
Therefore F G is not perpendicular to D E; and similarly neither is any other straight line except F C.

10
00:01:08,339 --> 00:01:12,260
Therefore F C is perpendicular to the tangent D E.

11
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:14,220
As was required to prove.

12
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:22,568
As Heath observes, Propositions Three Sixteen, Three Eighteen, and Three Nineteen form a complete logical triad governing circular tangency.

13
00:01:22,568 --> 00:01:35,705
Where the Porism to Three Sixteen proves that a line perpendicular to a radius at its extremity is a tangent, Three Eighteen proves the first partial converse: that the radius to the point of contact is perpendicular to the tangent.

14
